Reawakening OS 0.3.0-r10 — the rebrand: new name, new identity, two wallpapers

file:   reawakening-os-0.3.0-r10-amd64.iso
size:   933232640 bytes
sha256: 68e1bece9ab62a13aac8212966908e2556c758ab1afa026e50a01d4e4950ae12

Everything the user sees now says Reawakening OS: the boot menu (BIOS and
UEFI), /etc/os-release, /etc/issue, /etc/lsb-release, the hostname
(reawakening-pc), the session entry in the display manager, the welcome page,
the About panel, the window decoration and the ISO's own volume label
(REAWAKENING_OS) and publisher.

The two wallpapers ship in one place, assets/wallpapers/, and mkiso.sh installs
them into the image at /usr/share/backgrounds/reawakening/. The shell decodes
the PNG itself with the pure-Rust png crate — no image-processing dependency —
and paints every connected screen cover-fit at that screen's own size.
reawakening-default.png (Tux in a blue stained-glass window) is the default;
reawakening-moonlight.png (Tux on a moonlit beach) is chosen in Settings ->
Appearance, which shows both as thumbnails with an "In use" badge.

Verified in QEMU/OVMF from this ISO: boots to the desktop with the new wallpaper
and the bottom bar, the first-run welcome page opens and reads "Welcome to
Reawakening OS" with the tagline, the serial console greets
"Reawakening OS 0.3.0  reawakening-pc", the GRUB menu offers "Start Reawakening
OS", /etc/os-release and /etc/issue inside the squashfs carry the new name, both
wallpaper files are present, and 23/23 cargo tests pass.

Not verified: the real Chromebook, a real radio associating with an access
point, a real backlight panel, and the top-row media/brightness keycodes — all
unchanged from r9 and still hardware tests.

Internal names remain for compatibility: refurb-shell, refurb-control,
refurb-diag, refurb-keys, refurb-session, the /usr/lib/refurb paths and the
"refurb" desktop slot. The repository is still timdaymude/refurbished-linux.
Images up to r9 keep their published names and checksums.
